.TH TIMEDEXEC 1 "November 2026" "Custom Shell" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
timedexec \- run a program and terminate it if it exceeds CPU, memory, or wall-clock limits
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timedexec
[\fB\-c\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-m\fR \fIMEGABYTES\fR]
[\fB\-t\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-q\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR]
.I COMMAND
[\fIARGS\fR...]
.br
.B timedexec \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B timedexec
forks and executes
.I COMMAND
with the given arguments, then monitors it until it exits. If the process
exceeds a configured limit, it is sent
.B SIGKILL
and
.B timedexec
exits with status 124.
.PP
The child's CPU time and resident set size are sampled from
.I /proc/PID/stat
and
.I /proc/PID/statm
every 10 milliseconds. As a backstop,
.BR setrlimit (2)
is applied in the child before
.BR execvp (3):
.B RLIMIT_CPU
(limit rounded up, hard limit one second higher) and
.B RLIMIT_DATA
(twice the memory limit). Resource usage of the finished child is collected
with
.BR wait4 (2)
and printed as a summary.
.PP
At least one of
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-m ,
or
.B \-t
is required. Option parsing stops at the first non-option argument, so options
of
.I COMMAND
are passed through untouched.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " SECONDS"
Maximum CPU time (user plus system). Fractions are allowed.
.TP
.BI \-m " MEGABYTES"
Maximum resident memory. Fractions are allowed.
.TP
.BI \-t " SECONDS"
Maximum wall-clock time. Fractions are allowed.
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet. Suppress the status and resource summary printed to standard error.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose. Print the child PID and configured limits before running.
.TP
.B \-h
Print usage and exit with status 0.
.SH SIGNALS
.BR SIGINT ,
.BR SIGTERM ,
.BR SIGHUP ,
and
.B SIGQUIT
received by
.B timedexec
are forwarded to the child. The exit status then reflects how the child
terminated.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0..123
The exit status of
.IR COMMAND .
.TP
.B 124
A CPU, memory, or wall-clock limit was exceeded and the command was killed.
.TP
.B 125
.B timedexec
itself failed (bad arguments, missing limit, fork or setrlimit failure).
.TP
.B 126
.I COMMAND
was found but could not be executed.
.TP
.B 127
.I COMMAND
was not found.
.TP
.B 128+N
.I COMMAND
was terminated by signal N.
.SH EXAMPLES
Kill a busy loop after 2 seconds of CPU time:
.PP
.RS
.nf
timedexec \-c 2 ./cpu_hog
.fi
.RE
.PP
Limit a program to 50 MB of resident memory:
.PP
.RS
.nf
timedexec \-m 50 ./mem_hog
.fi
.RE
.PP
Combine all three limits and show details:
.PP
.RS
.nf
timedexec \-v \-c 5 \-m 256 \-t 30 python3 script.py
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use the exit status in a script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
timedexec \-q \-t 10 ./job; [ $? \-eq 124 ] && echo "timed out"
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /proc/PID/stat
Source of CPU time (utime + stime).
.TP
.I /proc/PID/statm
Source of resident memory.
.SH NOTES
CPU and memory limits are enforced by polling, so a process can overshoot a
limit by up to one polling interval before it is killed. Descendant processes
of
.I COMMAND
are not tracked.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR setrlimit (2),
.BR wait4 (2),
.BR getopt (3),
.BR proc (5),
.BR timeout (1)
